<section class="section section-muted">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Local rules</p>
    <h2>What Airway Heights actually requires</h2>
    <p class="lead">These are the city's written rules, not our house policy. A backyard stump and a street tree are different jobs.</p>
    <div class="rules">
    <div class="rule">
      <h3>Street and park stumps have to go below the surface</h3>
      <p><a href="https://www.codepublishing.com/WA/AirwayHeights/html/AirwayHeights12/AirwayHeights1234.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airway Heights Municipal Code 12.34.120</a> says stumps of street and park trees shall be removed below the surface so the top does not project above the ground. The city also keeps the right to plant, prune, and remove trees in streets and public grounds, and to decide which trees belong in the right-of-way (12.34.080 and 12.34.110).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule">
      <h3>Limbs over a street need eight feet of clearance</h3>
      <p>Section 12.34.100 says the owner of a tree hanging over a street or sidewalk has to prune it so it doesn't block a streetlight or an intersection, with at least eight feet of clear space. The city gives two weeks' written notice before it comes onto private property for a dead or dangerous tree.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule">
      <h3>Right-of-way work needs a permit first</h3>
      <p>The <a href="https://cawh.org/public-works-permitting/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">public works department</a> requires a permit before anyone grades, excavates, or otherwise disturbs a public right-of-way or easement. That is the planting strip and the highway frontage. It is not the middle of a fenced backyard.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule">
      <h3>A private-yard grind is usually not that permit</h3>
      <p>The city says landscaping, and tree or vegetation removal, needs a permit only if the excavation or fill passes the city's thresholds, or if the lot has a critical area such as a steep slope, wetland, or stream. The city page does not publish that threshold as a single number, so we don't invent one. If a stump looks like it sits in the right-of-way, we'll say so before we schedule. Questions on the threshold go to the city's planning department. Their page is <a href="https://cawh.org/when-is-a-permit-required/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule">
      <h3>We file the 811 locate</h3>
      <p>Utilities get marked before the wheel turns. Spokane Stump Grinder submits the 811 request as part of scheduling. 811 does not mark private lines such as sprinklers. When those are in the way, we flag it. Read the <a href="blog-811-locates.html">811 post</a> and the <a href="blog-private-locates.html">private-locate post</a>.</p>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The work</p>
    <h2>Grind it, or grind it and leave the lawn ready</h2>
    <p class="lead">Airway Heights is on the same list as the rest of the Spokane and Coeur d'Alene region. Two services. No flat rate posted, because a stump against a new fence is not the same job as one in an open yard.</p>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Stump grinding</h3>
        <p>The stump and the surface roots come down below grade. Chips go back in the hole and get tamped level. You can mow it. Most single stumps are a same-visit job. <a href="services.html">What's included</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Removal and cleanup</h3>
        <p>Everything in grinding, plus we haul the chips off and backfill with clean topsoil so the spot is finished level with the yard. This is the one to pick when something is going back in that spot.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Lawn repair after the tree</h3>
        <p>Renovating the lawn starts with the hole, not the seed. Chips left in the ground settle and sink. Cleanup hauls that wood out and leaves soil you can seed or sod over. We don't sell a separate lawn-renovation package, and we don't quote the grass.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="src">What moves the number is on the <a href="services.html">services page</a>: diameter at grade, root flare, depth, access, obstacles, what's in the wood, how many stumps, and whether the chips stay. Send a photo with the <a href="service-request.html">service request</a> if you can.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-muted">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">From here</p>
    <h2>We know which side of the highway you're on</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p class="lead">Born and raised in Spokane, Jeff brings more than 14 years of hands-on and management experience in home services, including four years as a business owner. Airway Heights sits just west on Highway 2, Fairchild off to the southwest and Spokane right behind you&mdash;a familiar part of the region he calls home.</p>
        <p>There's no downtown to meet at. The highway is the main street, and people already sort themselves by which side of it they live on. North is the houses: the older streets between Russell, Craig, and 1st, Sunset Elementary and the park in the middle of that, then the newer subdivisions. Sunset Elementary is Cheney schools. South is tighter, under the base, with Shorty Combs as the park on that side.</p>
        <p>Tell us the side and what's in the way. A gate in Traditions and a stump in the strip along the highway are not the same day, and you shouldn't have to explain the town to get a straight answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>North of U.S. 2</h3>
          <p>This is the side with the lawns. Sunset Crossing and Traditions sit north of 6th. Hunters Crossing is the newer one off Craig. A gate or a new fence is usually the first thing we ask about.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>The highway</h3>
          <p>The shops sit on Highway 2. A stump between the sidewalk and the curb is the city's, even when you mow it. We'll say so before anybody digs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>South of U.S. 2</h3>
          <p>Closer to Fairchild, and fewer new houses. If it's a commercial lot or the frontage, say so. Traffic and how we get in are what change the day.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Questions</p>
    <h2>Airway Heights stump questions</h2>
    <details class="q">
      <summary>Do you take stump jobs in Airway Heights?</summary>
      <p>Yes. Airway Heights is on the service-area list with the rest of the West Plains towns we cover. Call or text 509-434-9554, or send the details through the request form.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="q">
      <summary>Does my backyard stump need a city permit?</summary>
      <p>Usually the permit question is about the right-of-way, not the middle of a yard. Street and park stumps have to be taken below the surface, and disturbing the public right-of-way needs a public-works permit. On a private lot, the city requires a permit for tree removal when excavation passes its thresholds or the lot has a critical area. We will tell you if the stump looks like it sits in the strip along the street or the highway.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="q">
      <summary>Can you leave the spot ready to seed or sod?</summary>
      <p>Yes, if you choose removal and cleanup. Grinding leaves the chips in the hole, raked and tamped. Cleanup hauls the chips and backfills with topsoil, finished level with the surrounding yard. That is the setup for repairing the lawn. We don't install the seed or the sod.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="q">
      <summary>Who calls 811?</summary>
      <p>We do. The locate request is part of scheduling. You don't have to file it yourself. Private lines, including irrigation, are not part of an 811 locate.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="q">
      <summary>Why won't you list a price for an Airway Heights stump?</summary>
      <p>Two stumps the same width can be an hour apart once you add the roots, the fence, the depth, and whether the machine can reach the backyard. You get a written estimate for your stump before we schedule. Nothing on this page is a rate.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

